1 December 2026/१५ मङ्सिर २०८३ (मङ्गलबार, दिउँसो ३ बजे)
A Panel Discussion Facilitated by Ajapa Sharma
Teaching the Younger Generation: Experiences and Reflections
Dinesh Kafle, an academic, writer and journalist, faculty at Kathmandu University School of Arts; Aastha Guragain, an educator, writer and economic researcher, MSc from London School of Economics; Birsana Shrestha, Research Assistant at Martin Chautari and an educator under CTL fellowship at Thames International College

About the discussion
In this discussion, educators working with younger learners will share their experiences and reflections on the challenges and opportunities in their work. They will share what they find challenging about working with digital natives and whether, and how, AI has affected their work. The discussion will raise questions about effective teaching strategies and building more interest in the younger generation in the humanities and interpretive social sciences. The discussion also gives panelists an opportunity to reflect on how academics and educators can structure classes and discussions to encourage younger learners to engage with academic research and writing.&amp;nbsp;

About the Speakers
Dinesh Kafle is an academic, journalist, and writer from Nepal. He teaches Media and English at Kathmandu University’s School of Arts and oversees the university’s global engagement. Dr. Kafle has held several editorial positions over the past decade, including at Routledge Books, The Wire in New Delhi, and the Kathmandu Post. He also translates between Nepali and English.&amp;nbsp;

Astha Guragain is an educator, writer, and economics researcher with over five years of experience mentoring and teaching younger learners. She is a researcher for Photo.Circle’s international research project, “Strengthening Solidarities and Decolonizing Collaboration,” and is on the organization’s strategic planning team for the Common Futures Forum to be held in December 2027. Astha holds an MSc in Local Economic Development from the London School of Economics and teaches courses in economic development and technology at British- and American-affiliated universities in Kathmandu. She serves as a mentor and judge for the Nepal Economics Olympiad and has re-convened the Rethinking Economics chapter in Kathmandu. Astha writes broadly on subjects ranging from socioeconomics and power structures to art and literature.

Birsana Shrestha is an early career researcher at Martin Chautari. She is also engaged as an educator for the first year undergraduate student at Thames International College. She has published a book review in Samaj Adhyayan Journal and a reflective article in The Commons. Her area of work is focused on social science.&amp;nbsp;
